window.docContents[63].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Monad-Transformers--A-Monad-Construction-Kit--Exercises--Monad-Contract":{"contents":"Using pencil and paper, check that the rules of the monad transformer contract are satisfied for each monad transformer in this section.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monad Transformers\u0009A Monad Construction Kit\u0009Exercises","header":"6.2.6.1. Monad Contract","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Monad-Transformers--A-Monad-Construction-Kit--Exercises--Monad-Contract"},"/#more-inequalities":{"contents":"Lean's built-in proof automation is sufficient to check that  and  terminate.\nAll that was needed was to provide an expression whose value decreases with each recursive call.\nHowever, Lean's built-in automation is not magic, and it often needs some help.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance","header":"8.4. More Inequalities","id":"/#more-inequalities"},"/#updating-structures":{"contents":"Imagine a function  that replaces the  field of a  with .\nIn most programming language communities, this sentence would mean that the memory location pointed to by  was to be overwritten with a new value.\nHowever, Lean is a functional programming language.\nIn functional programming communities, what is almost always meant by this kind of statement is that a fresh  is allocated with the  field pointing to the new value, and all other fields pointing to the original values from the input.\nOne way to write  is to follow this description literally, filling out the new value for  and manually transferring :\n\n\n\nThis style of programming has drawbacks, however.\nFirst off, if a new field is added to a structure, then every site that updates any field at all must be updated, causing maintenance difficulties.\nSecondly, if the structure contains multiple fields with the same type, then there is a real risk of copy-paste coding leading to field contents being duplicated or switched.\nFinally, the program becomes long and bureaucratic.\n\nLean provides a convenient syntax for replacing some fields in a structure while leaving the others alone.\nThis is done by using the with keyword in a structure initialization.\nThe source of unchanged fields occurs before the with, and the new fields occur after.\nFor example,  can be written with only the new  value:\n\n\n\nRemember that this structure update syntax does not modify existing values—it creates new values that share some fields with old values.\nGiven the point :\n\n\n\nevaluating it, then evaluating an update of it using , then evaluating it again yields the original value:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOne consequence of the fact that structure updates do not modify the original structure is that it becomes easier to reason about cases where the new value is computed from the old one.\nAll references to the old structure continue to refer to the same field values in all of the new values provided.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Getting to Know Lean\u0009Structures","header":"1.4.1. Updating Structures","id":"/#updating-structures"}});